## _graph

# Optional typed entities + relations (see docs/research/TYPED_GRAPH_MEMORY.md).
# Relations are walked at query time, so naming a person in a question surfaces
# how they connect to you.
#
# - Person:Alex
# - Person:Sam
# - Sam -partner_of-> Alex
# - Project:ThisRepo -related_to_project-> Person:Alex
# - Document:budget.xlsx -about-> Event:Wedding
# - Document:budget.xlsx -in_service_of-> Goal:wedding_planning
#
# An entity can carry a short detail in parentheses. It is shown inline wherever
# that entity appears, so Chad answers "what city was my university in" from the
# relation itself instead of needing a separate fact:
#
# - Org:Northgate (in Corvallis, Oregon)
# - Event:Wedding (April 11 2028, Cedar Hall)
#
# Keep these short — they share the same per-turn budget as your prose facts.
# There is no point adding one to yourself: your own details are already in
# _core_profile, which Chad sees on every single turn.
#
# An edge can carry an optional validity clause — (since X), (until Y), or both.
# "until" means the relation ENDED: it is left out of normal retrieval and only
# comes back for an explicitly past-tense question ("where did I used to work").
#
# - Alex -employed_by-> Org:CurrentCo
# - Alex -employed_by-> Org:OldCo (since 2021-04, until 2024-09)
#
# Use employed_by (not member_of) for a job: it is single-valued, so telling Chad
# "I now work at X" automatically ends the previous one. member_of stays
# multi-valued for clubs, teams and groups, where several at once is normal.
